3:/**
4: * A mutable sequence with lazy half-open range updates.
5: *
6: * new RangeTree(values) copies a nonempty Array of finite numbers; otherwise it
7: * throws RangeError. length is read-only. Every range uses integer endpoints
8: * 0 <= l <= r <= length; invalid endpoints throw RangeError before any mutation.
9: * assign(l, r, v) replaces each value in [l, r); add(l, r, v) increments it.
10: * Both require a finite numeric v (even for empty ranges), throw RangeError for
11: * invalid v, return undefined, and leave empty ranges unchanged.
12: * sum(l, r) returns the sum (0 for an empty range); max(l, r) returns the largest
13: * value (-Infinity for an empty range). get(i) requires an integer 0 <= i < length
14: * and otherwise throws RangeError. firstAtLeast(lo, x) returns the smallest index
15: * i >= lo with value >= x, or -1. lo must be an integer in [0, length]; x must be
16: * a number other than NaN (infinities are allowed); violations throw RangeError.
17: * toArray() returns an independent Array in index order; neither constructor
18: * input nor returned arrays alias the tree. Arithmetic is ordinary JS Number
19: * arithmetic; callers must keep updated values and aggregate sums finite.
20: *
21: * Construction is O(n) time and space. assign, add, sum, max, get, and
22: * firstAtLeast are O(log n) worst-case; length is O(1); toArray is O(n).
23: * No operation scans or materializes a range to implement an update or query.
24: * Internal lazy tags apply assignment before addition.
25: */
